  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a progression of material structure from disordered to highly ordered states. A large arrow across the top indicates increasing structural order from left to right. At the left, the material is shown as an amorphous mass of tangled chains and irregular folds, representing a largely disordered arrangement. In the middle, a lamellar form appears, where chains seem partly aligned into stacked sheet-like layers while still retaining some flexible, wavy segments. At the right, the crystalline state is depicted as the most ordered, with straight, parallel chains packed into regularly repeating blocks.\n\nThe overall message is that these three forms lie on a continuum of order rather than being unrelated categories. The figure also compares which analytical methods can probe each region. A blue outer boundary suggests that solid-state NMR can access the full range, including amorphous, lamellar, and crystalline organization. A dashed yellow box around the more ordered middle and right regions implies overlap with diffraction-based methods, while the solid yellow outline around the crystalline region emphasizes that diffraction is most clearly suited to highly ordered structures. Overall, it contrasts disorder, partial ordering, and crystallinity while linking them to experimental accessibility.",
